2026-10-28T17:39:49.6194591Z {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgV3YCMfpATZVaqaki8NP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TL5Ggsu1tF75SkdfR5pff2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issue.json"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6008,"cache_read_input_tokens":24857,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6008,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5346e08b-8455-4198-ba98-d18ac85a106c","uuid":"c718bb35-b800-4745-968a-c220c74c61d5","timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:39:49.616Z","request_id":"req_011CgV3YBzbKQsyR9W6TXdUZ"}
2026-10-28T17:39:49.6417194Z {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TL5Ggsu1tF75SkdfR5pff2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"number\": 29,\n3\t  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/29\",\n4\t  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n5\t  \"title\": \"[New Resource]: aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement\",\n6\t  \"body\": \"### What new functionality are you requesting?\\n\\n* Service: Marketplace Agreement (`marketplaceagreement`)\\n* `aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement`\\n\\n### Description\\n\\nSubscribing an account to an AWS Marketplace offer is a console-only step today, so an account can't be built from Terraform alone when it uses Marketplace products. The case that hit me is EKS add-ons from Marketplace: `aws_eks_addon` for `datadog_operator` fails with `AddonSubscriptionNeeded` about 15 minutes into a new cluster's first apply, until someone accepts the offer in that account's Marketplace console.\\n\\nThe Marketplace Agreement API supports this for buyers now. `CreateAgreementRequest` (intent `NEW`) returns a quote, and `AcceptAgreementRequest` turns it into an agreement. `DescribeAgreement` and `GetAgreementTerms` read it back, and `CancelAgreement` cancels it. The SDK has it as [`service/marketplaceagreement`](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/marketplaceagreement).\\n\\nPlan, following the contributor guide:\\n\\n1. PR adding the `marketplaceagreement` service client.\\n2. PR adding `aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement` and its list resource. Create is `CreateAgreementRequest` + `AcceptAgreementRequest`, Read is `DescribeAgreement` + `GetAgreementTerms`, Delete is `CancelAgreement`. Every argument change replaces the agreement. The identity is `agreement_id`.\\n\\nWhat I found testing the API against a free offer (New Relic Kubernetes Operator):\\n\\n* `CancelAgreement` returns `ValidationException` with reason `UNSUPPORTED_ACTION` for about 80 seconds after the agreement is accepted, then succeeds. Delete has to retry.\\n* An account can hold one active agreement per offer. A second `NEW` request for the same offer fails with `UNSUPPORTED_ACTION`, so the acceptance tests have to run serially.\\n* `DescribeAgreement` returns the offer ID but not the agreement proposal ID the agreement was created from, so an import can't fill in `agreement_proposal_id`.\\n* `CancelAgreement` on an agreement that's already cancelled succeeds.\\n\\nAFAIK the acceptor can only cancel some agreements through the API (the buyer guide says usage agreements), so destroying a contract agreement will fail. That seems right to me: Terraform shouldn't be able to cancel a paid contract on its own.\\n\\n### Potential Terraform Configuration\\n\\n```hcl\\n# Datadog Operator EKS add-on (free)\\nresource \\\"aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement\\\" \\\"datadog_operator\\\" {\\n  agreement_proposal_id = \\\"at-6nuclnsh88uc2s8hs1mjzr5eu\\\"\\n\\n  requested_term {\\n    id = \\\"term-331241c92e99c39ff990052b4ae78d0165d892136e6af1aee8d55b9c28b540f9\\\" # LegalTerm\\n  }\\n\\n  requested_term {\\n    id = \\\"term-17e763eebddabad9921f51d9360900fb6fd07cd91a0ab8699859b40d2cd7109a\\\" # SupportTerm\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_eks_addon\\\" \\\"datadog_operator\\\" {\\n  cluster_name = aws_eks_cluster.example.name\\n  addon_name   = \\\"datadog_operator\\\"\\n\\n  depends_on = [aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement.datadog_operator]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### References\\n\\n* [Work with the Agreement API as a buyer](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/marketplace/latest/APIReference/work-with-agreement-api-buyer.html)\\n* [CreateAgreementRequest](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/marketplace/latest/APIReference/API_marketplace-agreements_CreateAgreementRequest.html)\\n* [`github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/marketplaceagreement`](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/marketplaceagreement)\\n\\n### Would you like to implement the enhancement?\\n\\nYes\\n\\n\",\n7\t  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n8\t  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n9\t  \"labels\": [],\n10\t  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:09:29Z\",\n11\t  \"as_of\": \"2026-09-26T18:23:51Z\"\n12\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5346e08b-8455-4198-ba98-d18ac85a106c","uuid":"83a1d273-b3b3-40fc-b5f7-6c52f44f79c1","timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:39:49.634Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issue.json","content":"{\n  \"number\": 29,\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/29\",\n  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n  \"title\": \"[New Resource]: aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement\",\n  \"body\": \"### What new functionality are you requesting?\\n\\n* Service: Marketplace Agreement (`marketplaceagreement`)\\n* `aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement`\\n\\n### Description\\n\\nSubscribing an account to an AWS Marketplace offer is a console-only step today, so an account can't be built from Terraform alone when it uses Marketplace products. The case that hit me is EKS add-ons from Marketplace: `aws_eks_addon` for `datadog_operator` fails with `AddonSubscriptionNeeded` about 15 minutes into a new cluster's first apply, until someone accepts the offer in that account's Marketplace console.\\n\\nThe Marketplace Agreement API supports this for buyers now. `CreateAgreementRequest` (intent `NEW`) returns a quote, and `AcceptAgreementRequest` turns it into an agreement. `DescribeAgreement` and `GetAgreementTerms` read it back, and `CancelAgreement` cancels it. The SDK has it as [`service/marketplaceagreement`](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/marketplaceagreement).\\n\\nPlan, following the contributor guide:\\n\\n1. PR adding the `marketplaceagreement` service client.\\n2. PR adding `aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement` and its list resource. Create is `CreateAgreementRequest` + `AcceptAgreementRequest`, Read is `DescribeAgreement` + `GetAgreementTerms`, Delete is `CancelAgreement`. Every argument change replaces the agreement. The identity is `agreement_id`.\\n\\nWhat I found testing the API against a free offer (New Relic Kubernetes Operator):\\n\\n* `CancelAgreement` returns `ValidationException` with reason `UNSUPPORTED_ACTION` for about 80 seconds after the agreement is accepted, then succeeds. Delete has to retry.\\n* An account can hold one active agreement per offer. A second `NEW` request for the same offer fails with `UNSUPPORTED_ACTION`, so the acceptance tests have to run serially.\\n* `DescribeAgreement` returns the offer ID but not the agreement proposal ID the agreement was created from, so an import can't fill in `agreement_proposal_id`.\\n* `CancelAgreement` on an agreement that's already cancelled succeeds.\\n\\nAFAIK the acceptor can only cancel some agreements through the API (the buyer guide says usage agreements), so destroying a contract agreement will fail. That seems right to me: Terraform shouldn't be able to cancel a paid contract on its own.\\n\\n### Potential Terraform Configuration\\n\\n```hcl\\n# Datadog Operator EKS add-on (free)\\nresource \\\"aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement\\\" \\\"datadog_operator\\\" {\\n  agreement_proposal_id = \\\"at-6nuclnsh88uc2s8hs1mjzr5eu\\\"\\n\\n  requested_term {\\n    id = \\\"term-331241c92e99c39ff990052b4ae78d0165d892136e6af1aee8d55b9c28b540f9\\\" # LegalTerm\\n  }\\n\\n  requested_term {\\n    id = \\\"term-17e763eebddabad9921f51d9360900fb6fd07cd91a0ab8699859b40d2cd7109a\\\" # SupportTerm\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_eks_addon\\\" \\\"datadog_operator\\\" {\\n  cluster_name = aws_eks_cluster.example.name\\n  addon_name   = \\\"datadog_operator\\\"\\n\\n  depends_on = [aws_marketplaceagreement_agreement.datadog_operator]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### References\\n\\n* [Work with the Agreement API as a buyer](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/marketplace/latest/APIReference/work-with-agreement-api-buyer.html)\\n* [CreateAgreementRequest](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/marketplace/latest/APIReference/API_marketplace-agreements_CreateAgreementRequest.html)\\n* [`github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/marketplaceagreement`](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/marketplaceagreement)\\n\\n### Would you like to implement the enhancement?\\n\\nYes\\n\\n\",\n  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n  \"labels\": [],\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:09:29Z\",\n  \"as_of\": \"2026-09-26T18:23:51Z\"\n}","numLines":12,"startLine":1,"totalLines":12}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TL5Ggsu1tF75SkdfR5pff2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
